"cells": [
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "1d7f76a2"
},
"source": [
"<p align="center"><a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-add_prefix.php"><img src="https://www.plus2net.com/images/top2.jpg" width="300" alt="plus2net Python tutorials">\n",
"\n",
"# Pandas add_prefix(): Name Columns and Index Labels\n",
"\n",
"Run cells in order in Colab. All sample data are synthetic and embedded. Save a copy in Drive to keep edits. Try the exercise before its solution. Explicit axis selection requires Pandas 2.0 or newer."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "5eebe599"
},
"source": [
"## Prefix DataFrame column labels\n",
"\n",
"For a DataFrame, add_prefix() prefixes column labels by default. Col_ changes NAME to Col_NAME, not to name1. It changes labels rather than values and returns a new DataFrame."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "b5f858ec"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"import pandas as pd\n",
"df = pd.DataFrame({"NAME": ["Ravi", "Raju", "Alex", "Ron", "King", "Jack"], "ID": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], "MATH": [30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80], "ENGLISH": [20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]})\n",
"result = df.add_prefix("Col_")\n",
"print(result)\n",
"assert result.columns.tolist() == ["Col_NAME", "Col_ID", "Col_MATH", "Col_ENGLISH"]\n",
"assert df.columns.tolist() == ["NAME", "ID", "MATH", "ENGLISH"]"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "88e3c351"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "  Col_NAME  Col_ID  Col_MATH  Col_ENGLISH\n", "0     Ravi       1        30           20\n", "1     Raju       2        40           30\n", "2     Alex       3        50           40\n", "3      Ron       4        60           50\n", "4     King       5        70           60\n", "5     Jack       6        80           70\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "bc244229"
},
"source": [
"## Prefix Series index labels\n",
"\n",
"For a Series, the row index labels are prefixed. This does not prepend text to the stored values or change the Series name."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "26247093"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"s = pd.Series([1, 5, 7, 9, 10], name="score")\n",
"result = s.add_prefix("Ser_")\n",
"print(result)\n",
"assert result.index.tolist() == ["Ser_0", "Ser_1", "Ser_2", "Ser_3", "Ser_4"]\n",
"assert result.tolist() == s.tolist() and result.name == "score""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "5a02d174"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "Ser_0     1\n", "Ser_1     5\n", "Ser_2     7\n", "Ser_3     9\n", "Ser_4    10\n", "Name: score, dtype: int64\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "4cc581e9"
},
"source": [
"## Choose columns or row labels explicitly\n",
"\n",
"axis="columns" prefixes columns and axis="index" prefixes rows for a DataFrame. The axis parameter is available from Pandas 2.0. Prefixing the index changes label-based selection and alignment."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "60d146cc"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"table = pd.DataFrame({"amount": [12, 20]}, index=[101, 102])\n",
"print(table.add_prefix("sales_", axis="columns"))\n",
"print(table.add_prefix("order_", axis="index"))\n",
"assert table.add_prefix("order_", axis="index").index.tolist() == ["order_101", "order_102"]"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "221cd25d"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "     sales_amount\n", "101            12\n", "102            20\n", "           amount\n", "order_101      12\n", "order_102      20\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "29bf4f0d"
},
"source": [
"## Do not confuse label prefixes with value prefixes\n",
"\n",
"To prefix values, operate on the column itself. Use a nullable string dtype to preserve missing values. For combining fields, see <a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-str-cat.php">str.cat()."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "a85f36a1"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"table = pd.DataFrame({"code": pd.Series(["0012", None], dtype="string")})\n",
"renamed = table.add_prefix("source_")\n",
"table["display_code"] = "SKU-" + table["code"]\n",
"print(renamed)\n",
"print(table)\n",
"assert renamed["source_code"].iloc[0] == "0012"\n",
"assert table["display_code"].iloc[0] == "SKU-0012"\n",
"assert pd.isna(table["display_code"].iloc[1])"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "1802f8ce"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "  source_code\n", "0        0012\n", "1        <NA>\n", "   code display_code\n", "0  0012     SKU-0012\n", "1  <NA>         <NA>\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "cc744e99"
},
"source": [
"## Prefix selected fields while keeping a shared key\n",
"\n",
"add_prefix() applies to the whole selected axis. Use rename() with a mapping when a join key must retain its name. Preserve one shared customer_id field and prefix the payload columns."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "f29e180d"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"sales = pd.DataFrame({"customer_id": [101, 102], "amount": [12.5, 20], "status": ["paid", "paid"]})\n",
"mapping = {column: "sales_" + column for column in sales.columns if column != "customer_id"}\n",
"prepared = sales.rename(columns=mapping)\n",
"print(prepared)\n",
"assert prepared.columns.tolist() == ["customer_id", "sales_amount", "sales_status"]"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "084249a2"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "   customer_id  sales_amount sales_status\n", "0          101          12.5         paid\n", "1          102          20.0         paid\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "8aa5c829"
},
"source": [
"## Validate labels before and after prefixing\n",
"\n",
"Prefixing does not repair duplicate labels. It also converts labels to string representations, which can collapse originally different labels such as integer 1 and string "1". Inspect <a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-dataframe-columns.php">columns before combining datasets."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "541c5a6e"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"table = pd.DataFrame([[5, 7]], columns=[1, "1"])\n",
"result = table.add_prefix("source_")\n",
"print(result)\n",
"print("Input labels unique:", table.columns.is_unique)\n",
"print("Output labels unique:", result.columns.is_unique)\n",
"assert table.columns.is_unique and not result.columns.is_unique"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "78e70eae"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "   source_1  source_1\n", "0         5         7\n", "Input labels unique: True\n", "Output labels unique: False\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "7ea97127"
},
"source": [
"## Practical workflow: combine named source tables\n",
"\n",
"This synthetic example joins one customer table and one order-summary table with one row per customer. Prefix each source payload while keeping the common key intact. Validate key uniqueness before merging; naming columns does not validate the relationship."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "27254aad"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"customers = pd.DataFrame({"customer_id": [101, 102], "name": ["Ravi", "Alex"], "status": ["active", "inactive"]})\n",
"summary = pd.DataFrame({"customer_id": [101, 102], "amount": [12.5, 20], "status": ["paid", "pending"]})\n",
"def prefix_payload(table, prefix, key):\n",
"    assert table.columns.is_unique\n",
"    assert table[key].notna().all() and table[key].is_unique\n",
"    mapping = {column: prefix + str(column) for column in table.columns if column != key}\n",
"    result = table.rename(columns=mapping)\n",
"    assert result.columns.is_unique\n",
"    return result\n",
"left = prefix_payload(customers, "customer_", "customer_id")\n",
"right = prefix_payload(summary, "sales_", "customer_id")\n",
"combined = left.merge(right, on="customer_id", how="left", validate="one_to_one", indicator=True)\n",
"print(combined)\n",
"assert combined.columns.is_unique and len(combined) == 2\n",
"assert combined["merge"].eq("both").all()"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "bb6dfe90"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "   customer_id customer_name customer_status  sales_amount sales_status _merge\n", "0          101          Ravi          active          12.5         paid   both\n", "1          102          Alex        inactive          20.0      pending   both\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "a09f3797"
},
"source": [
"## Check the output schema and retain the naming map\n",
"\n",
"Confirm that source-specific statuses remain separate and keys are preserved. The merge indicator exposes unmatched customers. Keep naming maps when downstream reports depend on a stable schema."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "be98b05e"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"expected = ["customer_id", "customer_name", "customer_status", "sales_amount", "sales_status", "merge"]\n",
"assert combined.columns.tolist() == expected\n",
"assert combined["customer_status"].tolist() == ["active", "inactive"]\n",
"assert combined["sales_status"].tolist() == ["paid", "pending"]\n",
"print(combined.drop(columns="merge").to_csv(index=False).rstrip())\n",
"# Optional Colab export:\n",
"# combined.drop(columns="merge").to_csv("named_customer_sales.csv", index=False)"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "a543d782"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "customer_id,customer_name,customer_status,sales_amount,sales_status\r\n", "101,Ravi,active,12.5,paid\r\n", "102,Alex,inactive,20.0,pending\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "2dd215d4"
},
"source": [
"## Avoid adding the same prefix twice unintentionally\n",
"\n",
"Repeated calls add repeated prefixes. If your process receives both prefixed and unprefixed labels, define a naming rule and check for collisions after conditional renaming. Do not blindly strip prefixes that may be meaningful."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "d8ea1b01"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"table = pd.DataFrame({"amount": [5], "sales_status": ["paid"]})\n",
"mapping = {column: column if column.startswith("sales") else "sales" + column for column in table.columns}\n",
"result = table.rename(columns=mapping)\n",
"assert result.columns.is_unique\n",
"print(result)\n",
"print("Repeated prefix on amount:", table[["amount"]].add_prefix("sales").add_prefix("sales").columns.tolist())"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "c1c86546"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "   sales_amount sales_status\n", "0             5         paid\n", "Repeated prefix on amount: ['sales_sales_amount']\n", ""
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "47c9a767"
},
"source": [
"## Choose add_prefix(), add_suffix() or rename()\n",
"\n",
"Use add_prefix() for an entire axis, <a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-add_suffix.php">add_suffix() for trailing label text, and <a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-rename.php">rename() for selective or arbitrary label changes. For row labels, consider whether changing them will disrupt <a href="https://www.plus2net.com/python/pandas-dataframe-loc.php">loc selection or alignment. These methods organise labels; they do not clean the stored values."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "40bf46e6"
},
"source": [
"## Common questions\n",
"\n",
"Does add_prefix() change cell values? No. Is there inplace=True? No; use the returned object. Why did my numeric index become text? The prefix forms string labels. Can I prefix only two columns? Use a rename mapping or select and combine deliberately. Does prefixing fix duplicate columns? No; check label uniqueness. Should I prefix join keys? Preserve the shared key or explicitly adapt the merge arguments. Does a prefix guarantee compatible data? No; still validate types and business relationships."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "8dbd24ab"
},
"source": [
"## Exercise: prefix only measurement fields\n",
"\n",
"Create a table with id, math and english. Keep id unchanged and rename the two measurement fields to exam_math and exam_english. Check that all values and the original table remain unchanged."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "433f2a81"
},
"source": [
"## Exercise solution\n",
"\n",
"Use an explicit mapping to leave the identifier untouched."
]
},
{
"cell_type": "code",
"execution_count": null,
"metadata": {
"id": "d3e9130b"
},
"outputs": [],
"source": [
"practice = pd.DataFrame({"id": [1, 2], "math": [75, 85], "english": [70, 80]})\n",
"answer = practice.rename(columns={"math": "exam_math", "english": "exam_english"})\n",
"print(answer)\n",
"assert answer.columns.tolist() == ["id", "exam_math", "exam_english"]\n",
"assert practice.columns.tolist() == ["id", "math", "english"]\n",
"assert answer["exam_math"].equals(practice["math"])"
]
},
{
"cell_type": "markdown",
"metadata": {
"id": "03135730"
},
"source": [
"Expected output\n",
"\n",
"text\n", "   id  exam_math  exam_english\n", "0   1         75            70\n", "1   2         85            80\n", ""
]
},
